@node Requirements
@section Requirements
@cindex requirements

@itemize @bullet
@item
Emacs 29.1 or newer.
@item
The @command{bd} executable on @var{PATH}.
@item
The MELPA libraries @code{transient} (0.10.1+) and @code{vui} (1.0.0+).
@item
@command{dolt} and @command{graphviz} for the full feature set and the
complete test suite.
@end itemize

@node Installing from MELPA
@section Installing from MELPA
@cindex MELPA

The package is published on MELPA:

@lisp
(use-package beads
  :ensure t)
@end lisp

@node Installing from Source
@section Installing from Source
@cindex installing from source

Clone the repository and add its @file{lisp/} directory to your load
path:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/beads.el/lisp")
(require 'beads)
@end lisp

@node Initial Setup
@section Initial Setup
@cindex initial setup
@cindex @command{bd} init

Initialize a Beads store in a project directory:

@example
cd /path/to/project
bd init
@end example

Then open the board in Emacs with @kbd{M-x beads}.  @code{beads.el}
finds the store from the buffer's @code{default-directory}; act on a
different project with the @code{:directory} argument
(@pxref{Store Scoping}).

@node The Shared Demo Project
@section The Shared Demo Project
@cindex demo project
@cindex shared fixture
@cindex fixture project
@cindex isolation

Every example in this manual runs against one canonical throwaway project at
@file{$@{TMPDIR@:-/tmp@}/demo-project} (by default
@file{/tmp/demo-project}).  The sibling @code{gascity.el} manual uses the same
path, the same fixed basename, and the same issue ids, so the two manuals read
as one documentation family.  The fixed basename is deliberate: @code{beads.el}
derives the buffer and mode-line name from it, so the screenshots and the text
you see in Emacs match.

Create, check, and remove the project with @file{fixture.sh} from the
repository:

@example
docs/manual/screenshots/fixture.sh create
docs/manual/screenshots/fixture.sh verify
docs/manual/screenshots/fixture.sh teardown /tmp/demo-project
@end example

@code{create} prints the project path and seeds it with
@command{bd init --prefix demo} followed by an import of the frozen dataset
@file{docs/manual/screenshots/fixtures/beads-fixture.jsonl}.  It is safe to
re-run: a project already at that path is recreated only when it carries the
@file{.beads-fixture-project} marker, and any other directory at that path is
refused rather than deleted.  @code{teardown} accepts only the exact shared
path, requires the marker, and succeeds when the directory is already gone.

@cindex fixture dataset hash
The dataset is frozen at twelve role-keyed issues, @code{demo-1} through
@code{demo-12}, with pinned ids, dependencies, labels, comments, and
timestamps.  Its @code{sha256} digest is
@code{6504c508bd25f3a2efc8a0e99bc940767b3306ae41352077fa446d2d6c319ca8}.
@code{fixture.sh verify} hashes the dataset against the single
@code{FIXTURE_DATA_SHA256} literal in @code{fixture.sh}, then checks that this
section and @file{docs/manual/screenshots/README.md} record the same value, so
a data edit cannot land without updating both documents.

@cindex fixture isolation
The project is never the repository's own @file{.beads} store and never a live
agent store.  Before every @command{bd} call, @code{fixture.sh} unsets the four
variables that can reroute it: @code{BEADS_DIR}, @code{BEADS_DOLT_PORT},
@code{BEADS_DOLT_SERVER_PORT}, and @code{GC_DOLT_PORT}.  Because the path is
shared and fixed, concurrent runs are serialized by an advisory
@command{flock} on @file{$@{TMPDIR@:-/tmp@}/demo-project.lock}.  @code{create},
@code{teardown}, and @code{verify} hold the lock briefly, and
@code{capture.sh} holds it for a whole capture run, so a second capture waits
(instead of sharing the store) rather than failing on the first run's
workspace lock.  The lock file lives next to, never inside, the project;
@code{capture.sh} exports @code{FIXTURE_LOCK_HELD=1} after acquiring it so the
nested @code{fixture.sh} calls it makes do not block against their own parent.

To follow along, create the project, open Emacs in it, and continue with
@ref{Getting Started}.  The @ref{Issue Workflow} chapter carries the
walkthrough that triages @code{demo-3}, unblocks @code{demo-4}, closes
@code{demo-6}, and inspects the @code{demo-10} epic.
